Based on the report and screenshots, the highlighter’s `TAG_NAME_RE` is too rigid: it first consumes one character, then only allows a “prefix + colon” if there are at least two characters before the colon. As a result `<a:OK>` never satisfies the look-ahead (because after consuming the single “a” it immediately sees “:”, which isn’t allowed), so the entire tag is never tokenized as XML.

To fix this, we can replace the old `regex.concat(...)`-based definition with a single regex that directly allows an optional `prefix:` of any length (even one character), followed by the local name:

```javascript
### src/languages/xml.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
    // Element names can contain letters, digits, hyphens, underscores, and periods
    const TAG_NAME_RE = regex.concat(/[A-Z_]/, regex.optional(/[A-Z0-9_.-]+:/), /[A-Z0-9_.-]*/);
    const XML_IDENT_RE = /[A-Za-z0-9._:-]+/;
=======
    // Element names can contain letters, digits, hyphens, underscores, periods, and optional namespace prefixes
    const TAG_NAME_RE = /[A-Z_][A-Z0-9_.-]*(?::[A-Z_][A-Z0-9_.-]*)?/;
    const XML_IDENT_RE = /[A-Za-z0-9._:-]+/;
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```

With this change:

- `<abc:OK>` still matches (prefix “abc:” + local “OK”)
- `<ab:OK>` still matches
- **and** now `<a:OK>` will match as well, because the `(?::…)?` happily accepts a single-character prefix plus colon.
